Give the Dex3's palm and fingers contact geometry, so a grasp is friction rather than a weld.

Generated against unitree_mujoco ae6a8403e272733e9996ef59990880330496177f, on top of 001-007.

Sixteen capsules and two boxes beside the existing visual meshes, sized from the meshes rather
than guessed, plus six excludes for the intra-hand pairs MuJoCo does not drop on its own. Capsules
and not the meshes themselves: a 15k-triangle fingertip hull is slow and numerically noisy at the
2 ms step this simulator runs.

contype/conaffinity 2 is a firewall. Every other geom in the robot and in every scene is 1, and
(2 & 1) || (1 & 2) is zero, so the hand pairs with NOTHING until a scene opts a prop in by setting
bit 1 on it. The walking policy was trained against a hand with no contact, so the flat,
navigation, LiDAR and perception worlds must keep the contact count they had; only the two
manipulation scenes set their props and table to 3.

group 3 keeps the primitives out of the viewer and out of the rendered camera image, both of which
draw groups 0-2, so perception still sees the meshes and nothing else. priority 1 makes the finger
parameters win outright instead of averaging with the object's, so no prop needs contact tuning.

Also rewrites dex3_visual's comment, which claimed finger contact would buy nothing.

diff --git a/unitree_robots/g1/g1_29dof.xml b/unitree_robots/g1/g1_29dof.xml
index 2ed4e3a..9b917ea 100644
--- a/unitree_robots/g1/g1_29dof.xml
+++ b/unitree_robots/g1/g1_29dof.xml
@@ -28,14 +28,26 @@
     <default class="dex3_motor">
       <joint damping="0.01" armature="0.001" frictionloss="0.01"/>
     </default>
-    <!-- Visual only, exactly like the rubber hand these replace. Grasping is modelled by
-         attaching the object to the arm in MoveIt's planning scene, so finger contact
-         would cost solver time every step and buy nothing until grasp physics itself is
-         under test. -->
+    <!-- The meshes, for looking at. They carry no contact: a 15k-triangle hull per fingertip
+         is slow and numerically noisy at a 2 ms step, so the gripping is done by the capsules
+         in dex3_collision below. -->
     <default class="dex3_visual">
       <geom type="mesh" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="1" density="0"
         rgba="0.7 0.7 0.7 1"/>
     </default>
+    <!-- Contact geometry for the fingers and palm, sized from the meshes. Capsules rather than
+         the visual meshes: a 15k-triangle fingertip hull is slow and noisy at a 2 ms step.
+         contype/conaffinity 2 pairs with nothing else in the robot or in any scene, so a world
+         that wants gripping opts its props in with bit 1 and every other world's contact set is
+         unchanged. Group 3 keeps the primitives out of the viewer and the camera render, which
+         show groups 0-2. priority beats the object's own contact parameters rather than averaging
+         with them, so no prop needs tuning. condim 4 adds the torsional friction a rubber pad
+         has and a held box needs. -->
+    <default class="dex3_collision">
+      <geom type="capsule" contype="2" conaffinity="2" group="3" density="0"
+        condim="4" priority="1" friction="1 0.02 0.0001"
+        solref="0.006 1" solimp="0.95 0.99 0.001"/>
+    </default>
   </default>
 
